  Note: 'public.printer_profile — §6, the physical device.

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ RECONSTRUCTED, NOT TRANSCRIBED. §6 of 17_printing.sql has not reached   │
│ me — both pastes of the file hit the 50,000-character message limit     │
│ before it. This model is derived from what the rest of the file states  │
│ about printer_profile plus the house conventions, so the field list is  │
│ a proposal. RECONCILE IT AGAINST §6 BEFORE GENERATING A CLIENT.         │
│                                                                         │
│ Load-bearing and safe (the file says so directly):                     │
│   * prefix prf_, table public.printer_profile                          │
│   * FK to fixed.device_master(dev_id) — "the counter, and its printer"  │
│   * it "asserts compatibility against" §3\'s page geometry, so it must   │
│     carry a paper code and dimensions of its own to assert with         │
│   * the full audit block, _is_active included (it is a master)          │
│   * NO is_default boolean — §5 owns that question, and the file is      │
│     emphatic that a second place to say which one wins is one too many  │
│                                                                         │
│ Inferred, most likely to differ from §6: everything from prfConnKind    │
│ down to prfCutSequence — the connection and capability block. The       │
│ vocabularies below are guesses at the CHECK lists.                      │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Declared here WITHOUT any CHECK constraint, as every other model in this
module is: the vocabularies live in the migration and in
PrinterProfileService.
prfConnKind     LOCAL | NETWORK | CUPS | WINDOWS | RAW_TCP | FILE
prfEmulation    ESCPOS | ESCP2 | PCL | PDF | RAW

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express, hand-authored in the migration:
ux_prf_code    UNIQUE (prf_company_id, lower(prf_code)) WHERE NOT deleted
ix_prf_device  (prf_device_id) — "which printers hang off this counter"
ix_prf_company / ix_prf_branch / ix_prf_created_by / ix_prf_modified_by
FK-covering, all partial'
}

Table print_log {
  plgId String [not null]
  plgAccYear String [not null, note: 'THE PARTITION KEY. One partition per accounting year.']
  plgCompanyId String [not null]
  plgBranchId String
  plgDeviceId String [note: 'The counter the render came from.']
  plgSrcModule String [not null, default: 'SALES']
  plgSrcDocType String [not null]
  plgSrcDocId String
  plgSrcAccYear String [note: 'The DOCUMENT\'s accounting year, which is not necessarily the year the
render happened in — a reprint of last year\'s bill is logged this year.']
  plgPurposeId String [not null]
  plgTemplateId String [not null]
  plgVersionId String [not null, note: 'The exact bytes. A real FK, and the point of the whole versioning design.']
  plgPrinterId String
  plgOutputMode String [not null, default: 'PRINT']
  plgCopyNo Int [not null, default: 1]
  plgCopyLabel String [note: 'ORIGINAL / DUPLICATE / TRIPLICATE — what the paper actually said.']
  plgLang String
  plgParams Json [note: 'The answers to ptv_params, ONE object for the whole render.']
  plgStatus String [not null, default: 'SUCCESS']
  plgError String
  plgPageCount Int
  plgByteCount Int
  plgDurationMs Int
  plgPrintedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  plgPrintedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master
  device device_master
  purpose print_purpose [not null]
  template print_template [not null]
  version print_template_version [not null]
  printer printer_profile
  printedByUser user_master

  indexes {
    (plgId, plgAccYear) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'public.print_log — §7, every render, immutable.

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ RECONSTRUCTED, NOT TRANSCRIBED. §7 of 17_printing.sql has not reached   │
│ me — both pastes of the file hit the 50,000-character message limit     │
│ before it. RECONCILE AGAINST §7 BEFORE GENERATING A CLIENT.             │
│                                                                         │
│ Load-bearing and safe (the file states each of these directly):        │
│   * prefix plg_, table public.print_log                                │
│   * PARTITIONED BY LIST (plg_acc_year), one partition per accounting   │
│     year, created by public.fn_create_printing_partitions(\'YYYY-YYYY\') │
│     as public.print_log_YYYY_YYYY. Retiring a year is a DROP of one    │
│     partition; retention is policy, not schema.                        │
│   * plg_version_id is a REAL FK to print_template_version — "what did  │
│     this bill look like when it was printed" is enforced, not          │
│     snapshotted and hoped for. This is the whole reason the body lives │
│     on the version rather than the template.                           │
│   * NO foreign key to any document. The subject is named by the        │
│     POLYMORPHIC SOURCE QUAD (module, doc type, doc id, acc year),      │
│     because the documents are themselves partitioned by their own      │
│     accounting year and a purpose may name a master or a report.       │
│   * plg_copy_label records what was printed on the paper. Deciding that │
│     the second print of a tax invoice is a DUPLICATE is a rule about   │
│     GST and belongs to the service layer, not here.                    │
│   * plg_params is ONE jsonb object per render — the answers to         │
│     ptv_params — not one per dataset. §3 cites this table as the       │
│     reason the declaration moved to the version.                       │
│   * A REPRINT IS NOT A STATUS TRANSITION. This table is the record of  │
│     printing; sale_bill.sb_print_count and its siblings stay a         │
│     denormalised cache of COUNT(*) over it, never the truth.           │
│                                                                         │
│ Inferred, most likely to differ from §7: the outcome block             │
│ (plgStatus … plgDurationMs) and the exact spelling of the quad.        │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

The composite primary key is the house shape for a partitioned fact table —
sale_bill does the same — because PostgreSQL requires the partition key to
be part of every unique constraint.

IMMUTABLE means immutable: there is no _is_deleted, no _modified_on and no
_sync_date here. Enforce append-only with a REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE on the
application role and, if you want it in the database, a BEFORE UPDATE OR
DELETE trigger that raises. Prisma cannot express either.

Declared here WITHOUT any CHECK constraint:
ck_plg_acc_year_shape   ^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{4}$ with consecutive years, the
same rule fn_create_printing_partitions enforces
ck_plg_src_module       the ppo_src_module vocabulary
ck_plg_output_mode      PRINT | PREVIEW | EMAIL | FILE | REPRINT
ck_plg_status           SUCCESS | FAILED | QUEUED | CANCELLED
ck_plg_copy_no          >= 1

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express, hand-authored in the migration:
ix_plg_subject   (plg_src_module, plg_src_doc_type, plg_src_doc_id,
plg_src_acc_year) — "how many times was this printed",
the query sb_print_count caches
ix_plg_recent    (plg_company_id, plg_printed_on DESC) per partition
ix_plg_version / ix_plg_purpose / ix_plg_template / ix_plg_printer /
ix_plg_device / ix_plg_branch / ix_plg_printed_by   FK-covering'
}

Table print_purpose {
  ppoId String [pk]
  ppoCompanyId String [note: 'NULL = shipped with the product, visible to every company.']
  ppoCode String [not null]
  ppoName String [not null]
  ppoSrcModule String [not null, default: 'SALES']
  ppoDocType String [not null]
  ppoCopyCount Int [not null, default: 1]
  ppoCopyLabels String
  ppoAllowReprint Boolean [not null, default: true]
  ppoSortOrder Int [not null, default: 100]
  ppoNotes String
  ppoIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  ppoIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  ppoSyncDate DateTime
  ppoCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  ppoCreatedBy String
  ppoModifiedOn DateTime
  ppoModifiedBy String
  company companys
  createdByUser user_master
  modifiedByUser user_master
  templates print_template [not null]
  assignments print_template_assignment [not null]
  printLogs print_log [not null]

  Note: 'public.print_purpose — §1, WHAT can be printed.

3.0 kept this list in C++ as a magic integer at ten call sites
(PrintUtil(this, 9) = Sales Bill). It is a table now, and that is the first
half of "printing is never controlled by code".

A purpose is (document type, what you want printed OF it): the same sale
bill is a tax invoice, a delivery slip and a godown slip.
ppoCompanyId NULL = shipped with the product, visible to every company.

Declared here WITHOUT any CHECK constraint, the same treatment
promotion_scheme gets: ck_ppo_code_shape, ck_ppo_src_module, ck_ppo_copies
and ck_ppo_sort live in the migration and in PrintPurposeService.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express, hand-authored in the migration:
ux_ppo_code    UNIQUE (ppo_company_id, lower(ppo_code)) NULLS NOT DISTINCT
WHERE NOT deleted. NOTE: NULLS NOT DISTINCT dedupes the
SHIPPED rows against each other only — a shipped
SALE_INVOICE and a company\'s own SALE_INVOICE coexist by
design, which is what makes forking (§2) possible.
ix_ppo_doc     (ppo_src_module, ppo_doc_type, ppo_sort_order), partial on
(NOT deleted AND active)
ix_ppo_company / ix_ppo_created_by / ix_ppo_modified_by
FK-covering, all partial on IS NOT NULL'
}

Table print_template {
  ptlId String [pk]
  ptlCompanyId String [note: 'NULL = shipped with the product. The only scope column here.']
  ptlPurposeId String [not null]
  ptlCode String [not null]
  ptlName String [not null]
  ptlDescription String
  ptlPublishedRevId String [note: 'The revision a render actually uses. NULL until something is published,
which is why a template with only a draft simply does not resolve.']
  ptlForkedFromId String
  ptlForkedFromRev Int
  ptlSortOrder Int [not null, default: 100]
  ptlCompanyKey String [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED: the owner with NULL folded to the nil uuid,
so that §5\'s composite fk_pta_template can point at (id, owner).
Read-only — writing it raises 428C9; the no-arg dbgenerated() is what
stops `migrate dev` emitting DROP DEFAULT.

It exists because MATCH SIMPLE treats a composite key as satisfied the
moment ANY of its columns is NULL, so a key on (ptl_id, ptl_company_id)
would stop checking anything at all for a SHIPPED design — exactly the
case the check is for. Folding NULL to the sentinel keeps every column
non-null, so the key always fires.']
  ptlIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  ptlIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  ptlSyncDate DateTime
  ptlCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  ptlCreatedBy String
  ptlModifiedOn DateTime
  ptlModifiedBy String
  company companys
  purpose print_purpose [not null]
  forkedFrom print_template
  createdByUser user_master
  modifiedByUser user_master
  publishedRev print_template_version [note: 'The circular half. Prisma needs both sides named; in SQL this is the
ALTER at the end of §3, because neither table can be created with a
foreign key to the other.']
  forks print_template [not null]
  versions print_template_version [not null]
  assignments print_template_assignment [not null]
  printLogs print_log [not null]

  indexes {
    (ptlId, ptlCompanyKey) [unique]
  }

  Note: 'public.print_template — §2, the design\'s IDENTITY, and nothing else.

Deliberately almost empty: a name, an owner, and a pointer to the revision
that is currently published. Everything editable — the body, the page, the
datasets — lives on PrintTemplateVersion, because it is versioned and this
row is not.

It carries NO branch, NO device and NO isDefault. Every one of those is a
RESOLUTION question and belongs to PrintTemplateAssignment. 3.0 split that
question across pgrp_style_id (never read by anything) and pstyl_default
(a boolean with no unique constraint), and both were wrong.

ptlCompanyId NULL = shipped with the product. A company customises one by
cloning it, which ptlForkedFromId/ptlForkedFromRev record — replacing 3.0\'s
pstyl_id < 5000 reserved range, a convention nothing enforced.

Declared here WITHOUT any CHECK constraint: ck_ptl_code_shape,
ck_ptl_not_own_fork, ck_ptl_fork_pair and ck_ptl_sort live in the migration
and in PrintTemplateService.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express, hand-authored in the migration:
ux_ptl_code    UNIQUE (ptl_company_id, lower(ptl_code)) NULLS NOT DISTINCT
WHERE NOT deleted. As with ux_ppo_code, a shipped code and
a company\'s own code coexist — that is what forking means.
ix_ptl_purpose (ptl_purpose_id, ptl_company_id, ptl_sort_order) INCLUDE
(code, name, published_rev_id), partial on active AND NOT
deleted — the "print in format" list.
ix_ptl_company / ix_ptl_forked_from / ix_ptl_published_rev /
ix_ptl_created_by / ix_ptl_modified_by     FK-covering, all partial

NOT YET ENFORCED ANYWHERE, and worth adding: fk_ptl_published_rev points at
ptv_id alone, so nothing stops it naming a DRAFT, a RETIRED version, or a
version of a DIFFERENT template. The fix is a composite FK on
(ptl_id, ptl_published_rev_id) against a UNIQUE (ptv_template_id, ptv_id)
on the version table; status still needs a trigger. Until then it is
PrintTemplateService\'s job.'
}

Table print_template_assignment {
  ptaId String [pk]
  ptaCompanyId String [note: 'NULL = EVERY COMPANY, the ladder\'s widest rung.

A global row can only ever name a SHIPPED design, and that is enforced
rather than trusted: ptaTemplateCompanyKey carries the template\'s owner
and ck_pta_template_scope ties the two together. Without it a global
assignment could point at one company\'s private design and every other
company\'s till would render it — their logo, their address, on somebody
else\'s paper.']
  ptaBranchId String [note: 'NULL = every branch.']
  ptaDeviceId String [note: 'NULL = every counter.']
  ptaPurposeId String [not null]
  ptaTemplateId String [not null]
  ptaTemplateCompanyKey String [not null, note: 'The OWNER of ptaTemplateId, carried so that the composite fk_pta_template
can exist. Same idiom as the promotion schema\'s benefit lock: a fact a
single-column key cannot check is made checkable by carrying the second
half of the parent\'s identity.

NOT NULL, and the nil uuid means "a shipped design" — matching
ptlCompanyKey rather than ptlCompanyId, so no NULL ever reaches the key
and MATCH SIMPLE cannot quietly stop checking. There is no default: the
writer must state which design\'s owner this is, because defaulting it to
the sentinel would mean defaulting to "shipped", and that is the one
answer that must never be assumed. PrintTemplateAssignmentService reads it
off the template rather than trusting the caller.']
  ptaOutputMode String [not null, default: 'PRINT']
  ptaPrinterId String [note: 'Where the paper comes out for this scope. NULL = the server\'s default
queue for the device.
Points at public.printer_profile(prf_id) — §6.']
  ptaPrinterName String [note: 'The same answer given as a bare queue or share name, for a scope whose
printer nobody has registered as a profile.

A FALLBACK, never a copy of the profile\'s name: ck_pta_printer_one_of
refuses it alongside ptaPrinterId. Two columns answering "which printer"
is the same defect §5 spent its whole argument removing from 3.0\'s default
handling, and a denormalised name on a MASTER goes stale the day the
profile is renamed — unlike plgPrinterName, which is a snapshot on an
immutable log and is meant to be frozen.

What it costs: a registered profile carries the family, the codepage, the
column count and what the device can physically do. A bare name carries
none of that, so a render through this column falls back to the counter\'s
defaults for all of it and prfPaperCode cannot assert anything — which is
exactly the check that stops an A4 invoice going to an 80mm roll.']
  ptaCopies Int [note: 'Overrides the purpose\'s copy count for this scope. NULL = use it.']
  ptaSpecificity Int [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED: 3 counter, 2 branch, 1 company, 0 every
company. Read-only — writing it raises 428C9. The no-arg dbgenerated() is
what stops `migrate dev` emitting DROP DEFAULT.

A single CASE rather than added weights, because a counter row must also
name its branch (ck_pta_device_needs_branch), so device 4 + branch 2 would
score SIX and every reader would have to know that 6, not 4, means
counter. Consecutive values cost nothing and cannot be misread.

Rung 0 is its own rung and not shared with rung 1: if a global row and a
company row both scored 0 the resolver would have a tie it cannot break,
and ux_pta_scope would let both exist.']
  ptaRemarks String
  ptaIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  ptaIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  ptaSyncDate DateTime
  ptaCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  ptaCreatedBy String
  ptaModifiedOn DateTime
  ptaModifiedBy String
  company companys
  branch branch_master
  device device_master
  purpose print_purpose [not null]
  template print_template [not null, note: 'COMPOSITE, against ux_ptl_id_company_key — (ptl_id, ptl_company_key) —
so that a company cannot assign another company\'s private template. Both
columns are NOT NULL on this side and generated non-null on the other, so
the key always fires; it subsumes the plain existence check a
single-column key would give.']
  printer printer_profile
  createdByUser user_master
  modifiedByUser user_master

  Note: 'public.print_template_assignment — §5, WHICH design wins, and WHERE.

THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO isDefault COLUMN. Default-ness is the ROW\'S
EXISTENCE. A pta_ row is the choice for one scope; templates that are merely
available are PrintTemplate rows matching the purpose, which is what the
"print in format" list shows.

That removes three defects at once:
* "exactly one default" becomes a plain unique index on the scope key,
with no flag anyone can forget to clear.
* THERE IS NO CLEAR STEP. Changing the default is an UPDATE of ONE row.
3.0\'s clear-then-set was scope-unaware, so on a mixed site setting the
Windows default silently unset the Linux one.
* A partial unique index on a boolean cannot be DEFERRED, so flipping two
rows in one UPDATE can fail on row order alone. With no boolean there is
no swap to get wrong.

Narrowest wins: counter -> branch -> company -> EVERY COMPANY, via
ptaSpecificity. Four rungs, not three: ptaCompanyId is nullable, so a
shipped design can be the default for every company that has not said
otherwise.

PAPER IS NOT IN THE KEY. Output mode is: a 3-inch thermal receipt genuinely
is a different artifact from an A4 tax invoice. Paper is a PROPERTY of the
chosen version, asserted against the counter\'s printer profile at render
time.

Declared here WITHOUT any CHECK constraint: ck_pta_output_mode,
ck_pta_device_needs_branch, ck_pta_branch_needs_company,
ck_pta_template_scope, ck_pta_copies and ck_pta_printer_one_of live in the
migration and in PrintTemplateAssignmentService.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express, hand-authored in the migration:
ux_pta_scope     UNIQUE (COALESCE(company, nil), purpose,
COALESCE(branch, nil), COALESCE(device, nil),
output_mode) WHERE NOT deleted — "exactly one default
per scope", as an index rather than as care. COALESCE to
the nil uuid rather than NULLS NOT DISTINCT, because it
is the same expression the resolver uses.
ix_pta_resolve   the render-time resolver, leading with the SAME COALESCE
expression and ordered by specificity DESC, so that
"this company\'s rows plus the global ones" is one index
scan.
ix_pta_company / ix_pta_branch / ix_pta_device / ix_pta_purpose /
ix_pta_template / ix_pta_printer / ix_pta_created_by / ix_pta_modified_by
FK-covering, all partial'
}

Table print_template_dataset {
  ptdId String [pk]
  ptdVersionId String [not null]
  ptdRole String [not null, default: 'DETAIL', note: 'MASTER: exactly one, the header context, one row read (3.0\'s pstyl_sql).
DETAIL: the repeating bands (3.0\'s printing_style_sql rows).']
  ptdDatasetNo Int [not null, note: 'THE BINDING — what a band actually points at. Unique per version.']
  ptdSortOrder Int [not null, default: 0, note: 'Display order in the designer. Binds nothing. Safe to reorder.']
  ptdName String [not null, note: 'The same binding by name, so a later engine can address \'items\' rather
than 2 and ptdDatasetNo becomes pure back-compatibility.']
  ptdLabel String
  ptdSourceKind String [not null, default: 'PROVIDER']
  ptdProviderCode String
  ptdSql String
  ptdSqlNorm String [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED: comments stripped, literals and quoted
identifiers replaced by tokens, casts flattened, lowercased. Read-only —
writing it raises 428C9. The no-arg dbgenerated() is what stops
`migrate dev` emitting DROP DEFAULT. EVERY sql guard reads this and not
ptdSql, so a keyword inside a literal is neither a false positive nor a
way through.']
  ptdRequiresCompany Boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'A report that is not company-scoped shows one company another\'s numbers.
Set false only for genuinely global data, such as a state-code list.']
  ptdParentNo Int [note: 'Nested detail, which 3.0 could not express at all: this dataset\'s rows are
the children of the current row of the dataset numbered ptdParentNo.

ptdLinkFields says HOW they match, as parent=child pairs, comma separated
for a composite — `sbi_id=line_id` or `sb_id=bill_id,sbi_slno=slno`. LEFT
is a column the PARENT dataset returns; RIGHT is a column THIS dataset
returns. Both are output columns, neither side is a bound parameter.

THE CHILD QUERY RUNS ONCE FOR THE WHOLE RENDER, NOT ONCE PER PARENT ROW.
It is bound with the same context every other dataset gets, returns the
parent\'s key as an ordinary column, and the RENDERER groups on it. A
14-line bill costs one query, not fifteen — which is also why ptdRowLimit
and ptdTimeoutMs measure the whole band, not a single child execution.']
  ptdLinkFields String
  ptdRowLimit Int [not null, default: 5000]
  ptdTimeoutMs Int [not null, default: 15000]
  ptdRemarks String
  ptdIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  ptdSyncDate DateTime
  ptdCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  ptdCreatedBy String
  ptdModifiedOn DateTime
  ptdModifiedBy String
  version print_template_version [not null]
  createdByUser user_master
  modifiedByUser user_master

  Note: 'public.print_template_dataset — §4, where the rows come from.

This model and PrintTemplateAssignment are the whole point of the module. A
layout has been data since 3.0; what was never data is WHICH QUERY feeds it.

It hangs off the VERSION, not the template. A dataset is part of the report
definition, not a setting beside it — if it hung off the template, editing a
query would silently change what every past version rendered, and print_log\'s
version reference would be a lie.

THE 3.0 FIX, in two columns:
ptdDatasetNo   THE BINDING. The body\'s bands reference this number. The
master is always 0.
ptdSortOrder   Display order in the designer. Binds nothing.
pssql_slno was both jobs at once — documented as a serial number, actually
the DataSet index — so reordering rows in the editor rebound every band to
the wrong query, with no error and no visible cause.

Declared here WITHOUT any CHECK constraint. That is a bigger omission than
usual, so the full list is below and PrintTemplateDatasetService must carry
every one of them:
ck_ptd_role                  MASTER | DETAIL
ck_ptd_source_kind           PROVIDER | SQL
ck_ptd_source_biconditional  PROVIDER <=> provider_code, SQL <=> sql
ck_ptd_master_is_zero        MASTER <=> dataset_no = 0
ck_ptd_dataset_no            0..99
ck_ptd_name_shape            ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$
ck_ptd_provider_shape        dotted lower snake
ck_ptd_parent                no self-parent, MASTER may not have one
ck_ptd_parent_pair           parent_no <=> link_fields
ck_ptd_link_fields_shape     parent=child, comma separated, no spaces
ck_ptd_limits                row_limit 1..200000, timeout 100..120000 ms
ck_ptd_sql_*                 the eleven stored-SQL guards, below

── THE STORED-SQL GUARDS ARE AN AUTHORING LINT, NOT A SECURITY BOUNDARY ──
The boundary is three runtime facts: parameters BOUND over the extended
protocol (which makes a second statement structurally impossible rather than
merely filtered), the query run in a READ ONLY transaction, and a role with
no write privilege. A CHECK cannot parse SQL and never will.

Verified behaviour of the guards against PostgreSQL 18, so the service layer
can reproduce it exactly:
REFUSED   \':iacc_year\' (3.0\'s quoted param), :1, arr[2:5], two statements,
data-modifying CTE, $q$dollar quotes$q$, nested block comments,
pg_sleep and friends, a query with no :company_id
ACCEPTED  \'last updated\' AND x = :company_id (a literal BEFORE a param —
the false positive the count-based check was written to kill),
\'it\'\'s\', a \';\' inside a literal, last_updated_on, updated_at,
E\'...\' and U&\'...\' (the comment claiming these are caught is
wrong; they normalise harmlessly)
KNOWN FALSE POSITIVES, both undocumented in §4 — the service layer should
give a better message than the constraint name does:
* a :name inside a "--" comment trips ck_ptd_sql_no_quoted_param, whose
error talks about literals
* a "--" inside a literal mangles the residue and the query is refused
by ck_ptd_sql_company_scoped, i.e. a company-scoped query is told it
is not company-scoped

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express, hand-authored in the migration:
ux_ptd_dataset_no   UNIQUE (ptd_version_id, ptd_dataset_no) WHERE NOT deleted
ux_ptd_name         UNIQUE (ptd_version_id, ptd_name)       WHERE NOT deleted
ux_ptd_one_master   UNIQUE (ptd_version_id) WHERE role = \'MASTER\' AND NOT deleted
ix_ptd_version      (ptd_version_id, ptd_sort_order) WHERE NOT deleted
ix_ptd_created_by / ix_ptd_modified_by   FK-covering, partial'
}

Table print_template_version {
  ptvId String [pk]
  ptvTemplateId String [not null]
  ptvRevNo Int [not null]
  ptvStatus String [not null, default: 'DRAFT']
  ptvEngine String [not null, default: 'JSON_BANDS']
  ptvBody String [not null]
  ptvSchemaVer Int [not null, default: 1]
  ptvPaperCode String [not null, default: 'A4']
  ptvOrientation String [not null, default: 'PORTRAIT']
  ptvWidthMm Decimal
  ptvHeightMm Decimal
  ptvMarginTopMm Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  ptvMarginBottomMm Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  ptvMarginLeftMm Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  ptvMarginRightMm Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  ptvColumns Int [note: 'Characters per line for the text engines. Meaningless for a page one.']
  ptvLang String [not null, default: 'en-IN']
  ptvFontFamily String
  ptvParams Json [note: '── What the OPERATOR is asked, once, for the whole render ─────────────
A JSON array, one entry per prompt:
[{"name":"from_date","type":"DATE","required":true,"label":"From date"},
{"name":"godown_id","type":"UUID","required":false,"label":"Godown"}]

ON THE VERSION, not on the dataset. The operator is asked ONCE — if
dataset 1 declared from_date as DATE required and dataset 3 declared it
as TEXT optional there is no answer to what the screen should ask — and
print_log.plg_params is one jsonb object per render, not one per dataset.

CONTEXT PARAMETERS ARE NOT DECLARED ANYWHERE. :company_id, :branch_id,
:acc_year, :doc_id, :user_id and :device_id are a closed set the server
holds the types for, and it finds which of them a query uses by reading
the query — exactly as ck_ptd_sql_company_scoped already reads
ptd_sql_norm for :company_id. So there is no "source" field here:
everything in this array is asked, everything else is context.

ck_ptv_params_is_array (jsonb_typeof = \'array\') is enforced in the
migration and in PrintTemplateVersionService.']
  ptvNote String
  ptvApprovedOn DateTime [note: 'Publishing is a privileged act: a version whose datasets carry stored SQL
is, in every meaningful sense, code.']
  ptvApprovedBy String
  ptvIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  ptvSyncDate DateTime
  ptvCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  ptvCreatedBy String
  ptvModifiedOn DateTime
  ptvModifiedBy String
  template print_template [not null]
  approvedByUser user_master
  createdByUser user_master
  modifiedByUser user_master
  publishedFor print_template [not null, note: 'The other half of the circular pair — the templates that publish this
version. At most one in practice; the schema does not say so.']
  datasets print_template_dataset [not null]
  printLogs print_log [not null]

  indexes {
    (ptvTemplateId, ptvRevNo) [unique]
  }

  Note: 'public.print_template_version — §3, the design itself, frozen once published.

THE BODY LIVES HERE, not on the template. That one decision is what makes
the rest of the module honest:
* print_log.plg_version_id is a real FK to the exact bytes that were
rendered, so "what did this bill look like" is enforced, not hoped for.
* Publishing and rolling back are a POINTER MOVE on ptl_published_rev_id
— one row, atomic. Rollback writes FORWARD as a new version, so the
history is append-only and a rolled-back design is still evidence.
* A render in flight cannot see a half-saved template, because a
published version is never UPDATEd.

ptvEngine declares what ptvBody IS. 3.0 had no such column — "the body is
QtRPT XML" lived in C++, so moving off QtRPT would have been a flag day for
every customer at once.

Declared here WITHOUT any CHECK constraint: ck_ptv_status, ck_ptv_engine,
ck_ptv_body_is_json (ptv_body IS JSON OBJECT when engine = JSON_BANDS),
ck_ptv_body_size, ck_ptv_rev_no, ck_ptv_orientation, ck_ptv_geometry,
ck_ptv_published (a PUBLISHED row must name an approver, the same shape
ck_prm_approved uses), ck_ptv_lang_shape and ck_ptv_params_is_array live in
the migration and in PrintTemplateVersionService.

Vocabularies, for the service layer:
ptvStatus       DRAFT | PUBLISHED | RETIRED
ptvEngine       JSON_BANDS | HTML_CSS | QTRPT_XML | ESCPOS_TEXT | RAW
ptvOrientation  PORTRAIT | LANDSCAPE

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express, hand-authored in the migration:
ux_ptv_template_rev    UNIQUE (ptv_template_id, ptv_rev_no) — NOT partial
on is_deleted, unlike every other unique index in
this module, because the history is append-only.
Note the tension with ptvIsDeleted existing at all;
resolve it one way or the other.
ix_ptv_template_recent (ptv_template_id, ptv_rev_no DESC) INCLUDE
(status, engine, paper_code) WHERE NOT deleted
ix_ptv_approved_by / ix_ptv_created_by / ix_ptv_modified_by  FK-covering'
}

Table _erp_seed_history {
  seedName String [pk]
  seedKind String [not null]
  checksum String [not null]
  lastStatus String [not null]
  lastError String
  durationMs Int [not null, default: 0]
  runCount Int [not null, default: 0]
  firstRunAt DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lastRunAt DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]

  Note: 'public._erp_seed_history — the seed runner\'s own bookkeeping table.

Created at runtime by `src/database/seed/seed-runner.ts` (CREATE TABLE IF
NOT EXISTS) on every boot with DB_AUTO_SEED, which is why it is declared
here as well: without a model and a migration, `prisma migrate dev` sees
the table as DRIFT and demands a full reset of every schema.

It holds no business data — one row per seed file, recording what ran, the
checksum it ran with, how long it took and how it ended.'
}

